.TH BCC09-COPT 1 2026-11-11 "bcc09 0.13.1" "bcc09 Manual"
.SH NAME
bcc09\-copt \- the peephole optimizer of bcc09 (copt)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I prefix\c
.B /libexec/bcc09/copt
.RB [ \-c\c
.IR char ]
.RB [ \-f\c
.IR input ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RB [ \-b\c
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-h\c
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-d\c
.IR dir ]
.IR rules ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B copt
reads assembly (the output of
.BR bcc09\-cc1 (1))
and replaces sequences of lines that a rule describes with that rule's
replacement, file of rules after file of rules, writing the result.
.B "bcc09 \-O"
runs it with the 6809's rules, rules.6809, which make code smaller.
It is Gero Kuhlmann's copt, from dev86.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f input
Read
.I input
(standard input by default).
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
Write
.I output
(standard output by default).
.TP
.BI \-c char
Lines starting with
.I char
are comments: they don't break a sequence of lines a rule matches
(and are dropped from a match).
.B bcc09
gives
.BR \-c; .
.TP
.BI \-d dir
Read the rules files from
.IR dir .
.TP
.BI \-b n
After a replacement, go back
.I n
lines before trying the rules again.
.TP
.BI \-h text
Write
.I text
as the output's first line.
.SH RULES
A rules file holds rules, each the lines to match, a line
.BR = ,
and the lines to replace them with, up to an empty line; a line
starting with
.B #
is a comment.
The rules are tried in order at each line of the input.
A rule's lines must match consecutive lines (a label, a line not
indented, ends a sequence); in them:
.TP
.BI % n
(a digit) matches any text, which the same
.BI % n
must match again in the rule, and which
.BI % n
in the replacement stands for;
.B %*
matches any text, to be forgotten.
.TP
.BI %[ a | b | ... ] n
matches one of the texts, as
.BI % n\c
\&.
.TP
.BI %![ a | b | ... ]
matches if none of the texts is there.
.TP
.BI %( expression )
matches a number equal to the expression, in which
.BI % n
is a variable's value; in a replacement it is the expression's value,
in hexadecimal.
A rule whose expression isn't a number (an address, such as a symbol's
offset) doesn't apply.
.TP
.BI %=[ a | b ][ c | d ] n
in a replacement, the text of the second list at the place where the
variable's text is in the first.
.TP
.B %%
is
.BR % .
.PP
A replacement can't be empty: an empty line there ends the rules.
.SH EXAMPLES
A store and a load of the same place leave the value in the register,
so the load goes (unless the address increments or decrements a
register):
.PP
.RS
.nf
ST%1	%2,%[X|Y|U|S]3
LD%1	%2,%[X|Y|U|S]3
=
ST%1	%2,%3
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
rules.6809's rules take every address for memory: a load straight
after a store to the same address goes.
A program that writes a hardware register and reads it straight back
(cc1 has no
.BR volatile )
gets the value it wrote, not the register's: compile that code without
.BR \-O .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 if a file can't be read or written.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bcc09 (1),
.BR bcc09\-cc1 (1)
